TASK 8: Capture Network Traffic with Wireshark
1. Objective

To capture live network traffic on a local Wi-Fi interface using Wireshark, apply display filters to isolate common protocols, inspect packet details, and record basic security observations.

2. Tools and Environment
Tool: Wireshark
Operating System: Windows
Network Interface: Wi-Fi
Capture File Format: .pcapng
Packets Captured: Approximately 1,399
Packets Dropped: 0, as shown in the displayed capture summary
3. Procedure
Opened Wireshark on a Windows laptop.
Selected the Wi-Fi network interface.
Started capturing live network traffic during normal network activity.
Applied display filters to isolate specific protocols and TCP traffic.
Selected packets and examined the packet-details and packet-bytes panes.
Observed HTTP requests, DNS queries, TCP connections, and TLS application data.
Captured screenshots showing the filters, packet lists, and packet details for documentation.
4. Display Filters Used
Display Filter	Purpose
http	Displays HTTP requests and responses.
dns	Displays DNS queries and responses.
tcp	Displays TCP packets.
tcp.port == 80	Displays TCP packets where either source or destination port is 80.
tcp.flags.syn == 1	Displays TCP packets with the SYN flag set, commonly associated with connection establishment.
tcp.stream == 0	Displays packets belonging to TCP stream number 0.
5. Observations
DNS queries and responses were observed, indicating domain-name resolution activity.
HTTP GET requests for /ncc.txt and HTTP/1.1 200 OK responses were identified.
TCP packets showed connection-establishment and acknowledgment activity.
Some packets were identified as TLS Application Data, indicating encrypted application traffic.
HTTP traffic can expose request paths, headers, and other content to parties capable of capturing unencrypted traffic.
HTTPS helps protect sensitive information by encrypting communications between endpoints.
Packet capture alone does not establish that a packet, host, or destination is malicious.
6. Security Notes
Prefer HTTPS over HTTP when accessing websites and transmitting sensitive information.
Avoid transmitting credentials or confidential data over unencrypted connections.
Use Wireshark filters to investigate unusual traffic and support network troubleshooting.
Capture traffic only on networks and devices for which you have authorization.
Protect capture files because they may contain sensitive network metadata or unencrypted application data.
